 ## Overview
-Stochastic Jittering is a sampling technique in direct volume ray casting that replaces regularly spaced sample positions with random offsets within each segment to eliminate banding and "wood-grain" artifacts [S1, S2]. Unlike [Uniform Ray Sampling](1261-uniform-ray-sampling.md), which produces visible structural artifacts due to aliasing, Stochastic Jittering transforms these artifacts into high-frequency noise that is visually less noticeable [S1, S2]. It differs from [Monte Carlo Volume Rendering](2060-monte-carlo-volume-rendering.md) in that it typically applies jitter only to the sample positions along the ray (often uniformly distributed) rather than using complex importance sampling or stratification for the entire integral, though it is fundamentally a Monte Carlo approach [S2].
+Stochastic Jittering is a sampling technique in direct volume ray casting that replaces regularly spaced sample positions with random offsets within each segment to eliminate banding and "wood-grain" artifacts [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Uniform Ray Sampling](1261-uniform-ray-sampling.md), which produces visible structural artifacts due to aliasing, Stochastic Jittering transforms these artifacts into high-frequency noise that is visually less noticeable [S1][S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Monte Carlo Volume Rendering](2060-monte-carlo-volume-rendering.md) in that it typically applies jitter only to the sample positions along the ray rather than using complex importance sampling or stratification for the entire integral, though it is fundamentally a Monte Carlo approach [S2, unofficial].
 
